S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=7 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by Bertie Stephens (born 1900), a breeder of hunting dogs, of Llwyncelyn, Llangeitho, Ceredigion; recorded 16.11.61 (St Fagans tape 428). He kept time as he sang by beating rhythms on the table with his hands, copying, he said, the sound of "y felin ban" (the fulling mill). He grew up in Abergorlech, Carmarthenshire, knew dozens of folk songs by heart and sang many of them in concerts. He heard this one around the time of the First World War from "Dai Arian Drwg", a man who sold watches round the fairs. The song was once especially popular in south-west Wales; the last verse is the ballad-seller's traditional appeal for money.
N:The bars alternate between 6/8 and 9/8, as in the source. The grace notes are small notes on the score: the E in "bryn-ais" splits the crotchet for a verse with an extra syllable ("I o-fyn am bris y cig"), and the B before the last "A mawr 'y ngholled i" is sung instead of A in verses 2, 4 and 5.
